# The environment variables the app reads. `.env` is gitignored and must stay that way —
# CI fails the build on a committed one, and on an API key pasted into any
# tracked file.
#
# The binary reads the plain environment -- there is no dotenv loading.
# Export these in your shell (or point your shell tooling at a private
# `.env`); deployed, they travel by `fly secrets set`.
#
# **This file is the whole list, and that is checked rather than promised.**
# `go/cmd/mtglab/configrecord_test.go` reads every quoted MTGLAB_/ANTHROPIC_/
# RESEND_/FLY_ name out of the non-test Go source and holds it equal to the
# names below, in both directions: a switch the code reads and this file does
# not name fails, and so does a name here that nothing reads. A switch nobody
# wrote down is a switch discovered in production, and a name kept after its
# reader was deleted is a lie an operator sets and waits on.
#
# Switches read only by `_test.go` files are deliberately absent -- they are
# not compiled into the binary at all, and the same test holds that boundary.

# Claude API key, from https://platform.claude.com. Read by the Anthropic SDK
# directly — the app never holds it in a variable, only asks whether it is set.
# Deployed, this is `fly secrets set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...` instead; see
# docs/HOSTING.md.
#
# Left commented deliberately. Anthropic's credential precedence treats an
# empty string as a *present* credential: `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""` selects the
# API-key path with an empty key and fails as a 401, rather than falling
# through to any other method or reporting no credentials. Uncomment when you
# have a key; do not leave it set to nothing.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# The SDK's other credential -- a bearer token rather than an API key. The app
# never reads either value; `claude.CredentialPresent` asks only whether one of
# the two is set, so a seat authenticated this way is as available as one with
# a key. Set at most one of them.
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=

# Which model answers, and how far a stance may be pushed. Both read at call
# time (`internal/claude`), so neither needs a restart.
#
# MTGLAB_CLAUDE_MODEL wins over the account's tier and over the house default,
# and it exists to run an A/B against a named model -- not so a deployment can
# drift onto a costlier default nobody chose. Unset is the right state.
#
# MTGLAB_CLAUDE_STANCE_CEILING clamps every request, including one from a
# client that asks for more; unset means Collaborator, the top of what the
# presets offer, which is what a local run on your own key wants. A value the
# stance parser cannot read fails **closed** -- a typo costs the feature rather
# than opening one -- so set it to a preset name or leave it alone.
# MTGLAB_CLAUDE_MODEL=
# MTGLAB_CLAUDE_STANCE_CEILING=

# What `mtglab ui` serves the shell and the tarot plates from. Both are also
# flags (`--web-dist`, `--tarot`) and the flag wins: these are the defaults the
# flags fall back to, which is how a container sets them once in its
# environment rather than in its CMD.
# MTGLAB_WEB_DIST=web_dist
# MTGLAB_TAROT_DIR=assets/tarot

# Where the pool and the decks live. Defaults shown; override to point a
# container at a mounted volume. `app.db` — users, sessions — sits in
# MTGLAB_DATA_DIR beside the pool, and is the half that must be backed up.
# MTGLAB_DATA_DIR=data
# MTGLAB_DECKS_DIR=decks

# Authentication. Off by default, which is what `mtglab ui` on a laptop wants:
# one person, no login. The deployment sets this, and every route except
# /api/health and /api/auth/* then needs a session. See docs/HOSTING.md §1.
# MTGLAB_REQUIRE_AUTH=0

# Who administers this instance (ADR 17). Set, the app reconciles the account
# at this address to an enabled admin every time it starts -- and every time a
# `mtglab users` command runs -- creating it unclaimed if it is not there. That
# is what makes "the maintainer is always an admin" a property rather than a
# one-time setup step: a demotion or an accidental disable is repaired by a
# restart, with no shell involved. No mail is sent at boot; claim the account
# from the sign-in page's reset link, or with `mtglab users invite`.
#
# Unset locally, deliberately: `mtglab ui` with auth off has no accounts at all
# and should not acquire one as a side effect of being run.
#
# Not a secret -- it is an address -- so deployed it belongs in fly.toml's
# [env] rather than in `fly secrets`. Guard it anyway: whoever can change it is
# an admin on the next boot.
# MTGLAB_ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com

# The login handle for that account. Unset, it is derived from the address'
# local part, which is a guess -- `ada.lovelace@example.com` becomes
# `ada.lovelace`. Set this when the guess is wrong, so the preference holds on
# every instance rather than only on volumes where somebody typed it into the
# CLI once.
#
# Used only when the account is created. It never renames an existing one: a
# handle appears in URLs and in `mtglab users list`, and changing somebody's at
# boot is a surprise nothing can warn them about.
#
# There is no MTGLAB_ADMIN_PASSWORD, and there will not be one. ADR 16: no
# password is ever chosen by one person for another, and a password in the
# environment is a password in `fly secrets list`, in a process listing, and in
# whatever file you pasted it into. The bootstrapped account is unclaimed --
# claim it from the sign-in page's reset link.
# MTGLAB_ADMIN_USERNAME=you

# Whether the session cookie carries `Secure`. Follows MTGLAB_REQUIRE_AUTH
# unless set — auth on means deployed means TLS. Set it to 0 only if you are
# running with auth on over plain HTTP, which you should not be doing.
# MTGLAB_SECURE_COOKIES=

# Transactional email, for invite links and password resets (ADR 16). Get a key
# from https://resend.com; deployed, this is `fly secrets set RESEND_API_KEY=...`.
#
# Without it the app prints messages to the console instead of sending them,
# which is what you want on a laptop -- `mtglab users invite` shows you the link
# it would have mailed. With MTGLAB_REQUIRE_AUTH on, that fallback is REFUSED
# rather than used: printing recipients into a deployment's log is exactly what
# ADR 16 forbids, so a deployment without a key fails loudly instead.
# RESEND_API_KEY=

# The From address on those messages. Resend refuses anything outside a domain
# you have verified with it, so this is a deploy-day setting rather than a
# preference. Only the console sender ever uses the default.
# MTGLAB_EMAIL_FROM=sylvan-library <no-reply@example.com>

# Where this instance answers, for links that have to work days later in
# somebody's inbox. Never derived from the request: a Host header is
# client-supplied, and building a password-reset link out of one is how the
# link ends up pointing at somebody else's domain. Defaults to the `mtglab ui`
# port, so a laptop needs nothing here and a deployment that forgets it sends
# links to localhost -- visibly broken rather than quietly hijackable.
# MTGLAB_BASE_URL=https://mtglab.example.com

# The header naming the real client address, for login rate limiting. Unset,
# nothing is trusted and the socket address is used. Set this ONLY when a proxy
# you control overwrites the header on the way in — otherwise a client picks
# its own rate-limit bucket. On Fly, that header is `Fly-Client-IP`.
# MTGLAB_CLIENT_IP_HEADER=

# The Admin page's far-seeing glass: Fly's managed Prometheus, read-only.
# Mint it yourself -- `fly tokens create readonly` -- because a credential is
# a human act; deployed, this is `fly secrets set FLY_METRICS_TOKEN=...`.
#
# Unset, the panel is ABSENT rather than empty: a laptop has no Fly org and
# should not be shown a broken-looking widget. The rest of the dashboard is
# the box's own account of itself and needs nothing here. Read straight from
# the environment, never through `config`: `internal/config`'s package comment
# is the argument -- a value we never hold is a value we cannot log.
# FLY_METRICS_TOKEN=

# Which organisation and app the query above asks about. Both default to this
# project's own (`personal` / `sylvan-library`), so a fork sets them and this
# instance never needs to.
# FLY_ORG_SLUG=personal
# FLY_APP_NAME=sylvan-library

# ---- The Coliseum at Night (ADR 46) -----------------------------------------

# When the arena opens after dark: a wall-clock window "HH:MM-HH:MM" in the
# zone below, and it may cross midnight ("23:30-01:30"). Unset means no
# scheduled nights -- the admin's sample trigger still works, which is how the
# first real window gets chosen: measure an hour, count the bouts, then set
# this. All five values are read at boot into the served configuration; a
# malformed window, an unknown zone, or games outside 1..20 refuse the boot
# with a sentence rather than running the night on a guess.
# MTGLAB_NIGHT_WINDOW=

# Whose wall the clock above hangs on: an IANA zone name, required whenever
# the window is set. The friends this instance serves are not all in one zone,
# so there is deliberately no default.
# MTGLAB_NIGHT_ZONE=America/Los_Angeles

# How much night there is to spend: the cap on bouts in one scheduled night
# (default 6), and one account's share of it (default 2) -- ADR 46's fairness
# rule, so nobody's forty decks own the window. Sample runs ignore both; their
# deadline is the only bound, because a measurement wants saturation.
# MTGLAB_NIGHT_BOUTS=6
# MTGLAB_NIGHT_BOUTS_PER_ACCOUNT=2

# Games per bout, 1 to 20 -- the same ceiling the Coliseum's door enforces on
# an interactive match. Default 10.
# MTGLAB_NIGHT_GAMES=10

# ---- Tier 3 on the hosted worker (ADR 35) -----------------------------------

# The dial: with this set (and the token below present), `/api/forge` answers
# yes and matches run on the `forge-worker` machine instead of a local JVM.
# A laptop leaves all of this unset and plays against its own Forge install.
# MTGLAB_FORGE_WORKER=1

# A Fly deploy token for the Machines API -- `fly tokens create deploy -a
# sylvan-library`. Deployed, this is `fly secrets set MTGLAB_FLY_API_TOKEN=...`
# and never a line in fly.toml: the repository is public. Read straight from
# the environment, never through `config`, like every credential here.
# MTGLAB_FLY_API_TOKEN=

# The bearer token the worker's shim requires when set. Set it as an app
# secret and both sides arm at once -- one app's secrets reach every machine
# in it. Unset, the shim answers anyone on the private network.
# MTGLAB_FORGE_SHIM_TOKEN=

# Point straight at a running shim and skip the Machines API entirely. This
# is how the test suite drives the real HTTP path, and how a laptop can talk
# to a shim started by hand (`mtglab forge-shim`).
# MTGLAB_FORGE_WORKER_URL=

# Which Fly app and which machine in it. Fly injects FLY_APP_NAME into every
# machine it runs, so the deployed process needs neither of these; the
# override is for talking to the instance from a laptop, and for tests.
# Unset, the machine is `forge-worker`.
# MTGLAB_FLY_APP=sylvan-library
# MTGLAB_FORGE_MACHINE=forge-worker

# The shim's own door, read by `mtglab forge-shim` on the worker. Host unset
# means `::` -- every interface, which on Fly is the private network only.
# MTGLAB_FORGE_SHIM_PORT is read by **both** sides: the shim listens on it and
# the app dials it, so a change to one is a change to the other.
#
# MTGLAB_FORGE_IDLE_SECONDS is quiet time before the shim exits and stops its
# own machine -- three minutes by default, and `0` means never, which is what
# a shim started by hand on a laptop wants. MTGLAB_FORGE_MEMORY_MB is the JVM
# heap ceiling, deliberately below the local default because the worker
# machine has 4GB total and the heap is not the only resident.
# MTGLAB_FORGE_SHIM_HOST=
# MTGLAB_FORGE_SHIM_PORT=8080
# MTGLAB_FORGE_IDLE_SECONDS=180
# MTGLAB_FORGE_MEMORY_MB=3072

# ---- Tier 3 against a local Forge -------------------------------------------

# Where Forge is unpacked, which JVM runs it, and where it keeps its own user
# data. All three are resolved when something reaches for them, so installing
# Forge mid-session needs no restart.
#
# MTGLAB_FORGE_HOME is the one path in this project that points outside both
# the checkout and the data directory -- nothing under it is ever tracked.
# Unset, it is `~/.local/share/mtglab/forge`.
#
# MTGLAB_JAVA beats the JDK unpacked beside the distribution, which beats
# whatever `java` is on PATH; each candidate's version is probed rather than
# trusted, and anything below the required major is skipped.
#
# MTGLAB_FORGE_PROFILE keeps generated decks out of the Forge profile a person
# saves their own decks into. Unset, it is `~/.local/share/mtglab/forge-profile`.
# MTGLAB_FORGE_HOME=
# MTGLAB_JAVA=
# MTGLAB_FORGE_PROFILE=
#
# MTGLAB_SCRIBE_CLASSES points at the scribe's compiled classes (ADR 42) -- the
# listener on Forge's event bus that reports the board a game log has no
# category for. `Dockerfile.forge` builds them and sets this to /opt/scribe;
# a laptop sets it to the checkout's `scribe/out` after running
# `scribe/build.sh`. Unset, matches play through Forge's own `sim` and are
# narrated from the log, with no board -- which is what every worker did before
# the scribe existed and what one deployed without it still does.
# MTGLAB_SCRIBE_CLASSES=
